<div class="mx-auto max-w-md pb-12">
  <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between">
    <h1 class="text-3xl text-gray-900">
      <%= @workout[:date].strftime('%A %b %-d') %>
    </h1>
    <a href="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>" class="text-sm text-gray-500 underline hover:text-lime-500">record</a>
  </div>

  <div id="session-body">
    <%= render('workouts/_session_body') %>
  </div>
</div>

<%# The lifts are a horizontal scroller now, and htmx replaces the whole of #session-body
    after every tap. An innerHTML swap destroys the scroller along with everything else in
    there, so its scrollLeft goes with it: without this, finishing a set on lift three
    drops the lifter back on lift one, every set, all session. That is the one thing that
    would have made the panels unusable.

    hx-preserve is the obvious answer and it is the wrong one: it keeps the old element,
    which means it keeps the old element's children, so the set that was just completed
    would never redraw. Re-rendering the panels is the entire point of the swap. What has
    to survive is one number, and only that.

    It is a listener on document.body rather than on the scroller because htmx events
    bubble, so this binds once when the page loads and keeps working across every swap.
    Binding to the scroller itself -- or writing the gesture in JS at all -- would mean
    re-binding after each swap to an element that no longer exists, which is the class of
    bug that only shows up mid-session with a bar in your hands.

    The offset is put back raw rather than recomputed from a lift index. Every panel is
    exactly the scroller's own width, so the offset before the swap already names a snap
    point, and the browser re-snaps to it anyway if a swipe was in flight.

    None of this runs without JavaScript and none of it is wanted: Tailwind arrives from
    the play CDN, which is a script, so with JS off there is no flex row and no snapping
    either. The lifts are plain blocks in source order, the forms post normally and the
    route redirects, which is the page this screen has always degraded to. %>
<script>
  (function () {
    var offset = 0;
    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:beforeSwap', function () {
      var panels = document.getElementById('lift-panels');
      if (panels) { offset = panels.scrollLeft; }
    });
    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterSwap', function () {
      var panels = document.getElementById('lift-panels');
      if (panels) { panels.scrollLeft = offset; }
    });
  })();
</script>
